/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Write the system as y = T{x}, where T is the operator, x is the input signal and y is the output signal.
   - x₁ and x₂ are any two input signals; a and b are any constants (real or complex); T{x₁} and T{x₂} are the outputs for those inputs.
   - The formula packs two rules.
   - Linearity exists so that a complicated input can be split into simple pieces, each piece pushed through the system, and the outputs added.
   - A quick test: a linear system must give zero output for zero input, because T{0} = T{0·x} = 0·T{x} = 0.
   - Time invariance is about shifts, not about the system ageing.
   - t₀ is the delay in seconds for continuous time (CT); n₀ is the delay in samples for discrete time (DT); y is the output produced by x.
   - Causality: the output at a chosen instant t₀ may depend only on input values at times t ≤ t₀, never on future input.
   - For an LTI system, causality and BIBO stability both reduce to tests on the impulse response h(t).
   - BIBO means bounded-input bounded-output.
*/
