/* book exam traps: Time invariance does not mean the system never changes with time. It means a shift in the input produces exactly that shift in the output. The coefficient t in t x(t) makes the system time-varying. | A constant added term breaks linearity. For y(t) = x(t) + 2, the zero input gives 2, not 0, so the superposition formula fails no matter how small the constant is. | Both linearity conditions are needed. Check scaling and additivity separately, because a system that fails either one is not linear. Example: y(t) = x²(t) fails scaling (T{a x} = a²x², not a x²) and fails additivity ((x₁ + x₂)² ≠ x₁² + x₂²). | Causality is about which input samples are used, not about how fast the output appears. A system using x(t + 1) is non-causal even if its output is delayed by an hour. | For LTI stability, take the absolute value inside the integral or sum. ∫h(t)dt finite does not guarantee stability; ∫|h(t)|dt must be finite. */
